WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.103
Manhattan attorney who investigated

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:03.670
Donald Trump

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.138
and his organization's business

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.306
dealings is speaking out.

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:06.973
Frustrated.

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:07.741
The former president

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:09.709
was not indicted in that case.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:10.744
Now, in his new book,

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.612
the former prosecutor Mark Pomerantz

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.180
says the evidence he had access

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:14.714
to would have

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.383
easily resulted in charges

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:18.551
against anyone else.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:21.388
Take the exact same conduct

00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:23.523
and make it not about Donald Trump

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.091
and not about a former president

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.326
of the United States.

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:28.294
With the case have been indicted.

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.229
It would have been indicted

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:31.164
in a flat second.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:32.032
Bring in CNN's

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:32.732
Chris Cornell,

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:34.501
who's tracking this investigation Carol.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:35.668
That's pretty strong words

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:36.603
from the prosecutor, Mr.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:38.405
Palmer, as a former prosecutor.

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:39.272
What critical points did

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:40.907
he make in this interview?

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:41.808
Yeah,
so this was his

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:43.143
first television interview

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.211
since he resigned in protest last year

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.613
after the incoming D.A.,

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:48.715
Alvin Bragg, stopped him from pursuing

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:49.449
an indictment

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:50.850
against the former president.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:51.718
Now, in this interview,

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:52.952
Pomeranz also says

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:53.920
that refusing

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.422
to move forward with those charges

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:57.390
was a grave failure of justice.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.059
He said he believed that they had bits

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:00.260
and pieces of evidence

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.461
that would tie Trump

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:03.430
to these alleged crimes.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.331
Now, he was he did not.

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:06.533
That was not a universal view

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:07.634
within the DA's office.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:08.701
Several prosecutors

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:09.803
resigned from the team

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:10.904
because they thought they were moving

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.005
too far ahead.

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.340
And, you know, sources told us last year

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:16.443
and in addition, other experienced

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:18.211
career prosecutors on that team

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.312
also thought that they didn't

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.314
have enough evidence to prove

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:21.981
that Trump

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.516
had criminal intent

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:24.451
in the preparation

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:25.985
of these financial statements.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.087
And now some of these bits

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.021
and pieces of evidence

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:28.855
have come out

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:29.756
in the New York attorney

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.057
general's civil lawsuit

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:32.392
against the former president

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:33.760
and his family members.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.360
You know,
in that

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.628
she alleged that they had engaged

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:36.729
in a decade long fraud

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.998
by manipulating the value of many assets

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:40.800
on the company's financial statements.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.401
And that Trumps

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:43.403
have denied any wrongdoing.

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.839
But you know that Bragg has said that

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:47.974
he believes those career prosecutors

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.076
he thought more work needed to be done.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.177
And he said that Pomerantz

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.213
his plane was not ready for takeoff.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:54.414
Now, the former president's

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:55.949
attorney has said that POMERANTZ

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.051
the statements are a desperate attempt

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:00.053
to sell this book.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:00.720
And one thing

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.088
that Bragg and Trump agree on

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.523
is that no one wanted this book

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:05.058
published, John.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.693
The prosecutor certainly doesn't want it

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:07.360
published in the Middle

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.761
of an ongoing investigation.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.130
Carol is grateful

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:10.997
for the important reporting.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:12.031
Let's get some perspective.

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:12.599
CNN legal

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:13.500
national security analyst

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.734
Carrie Cordero is back with us.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:15.435
And let's listen

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.070
because in the 60 Minutes interview,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.872
Bill Whitaker asked a key question

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.306
How strong is the evidence

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:21.608
tying this to Donald Trump?

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:23.910
So what

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.247
ties Donald Trump directly to this?

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:28.381
You know, couldn't

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.650
he say, my accountant said

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.318
it's worth this, I signed it.

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.653
There were

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:36.723
many bits and pieces of evidence

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.758
on which we could rely

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.161
in making that case.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.362
It's an interesting choice

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:43.963
of words, many bits and pieces.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:45.331
Normally in the case,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.032
you hear prosecutors

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:47.567
say the evidence was overwhelming

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.869
or it was a slam dunk. Bits and pieces.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:50.370
You bring it.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.438
Do you bring a case against anyone

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.374
but or especially the former president,

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.475
if you have bits and pieces. Yeah.

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:56.409
Normally, prosecutors

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:57.443
would not get too excited

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.112
about bringing a case based on bits

00:02:59.112 --> 00:02:59.612
and pieces.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.014
What they want to be able to do

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.048
is bring an indictment

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.517
based on a case that they are

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.719
have a large amount of evidence

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.155
that they feel can convince a jury.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:11.124
And it's really not ethically responsible

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:11.925
to bring a case

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:13.293
unless they have confidence

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.495
that they can actually win it.

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.097
And there's a real irony here as well.

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.900
Presumably, this case is still ongoing

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.303
in some way in New York.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.238
And the fact that a prosecutor

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:27.240
who has knowledge and access

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.075
to what the evidence was

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.411
is now publishing a book

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:33.580
and presumably profiting off of

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.281
that inside information

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.649
for an ongoing case,

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:37.784
not a case that is over,

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:38.551
has been disposed

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.053
of, was prosecuted in the past.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:40.587
And, you know,

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:41.888
prosecutors write about the cases

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.256
that I did way back when.

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:44.490
This is very different.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.025
This is something that's ongoing.

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.093
So I think there's a real

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:48.828
sort of ethical issue there as well.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.196
And to that ethical issue

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:51.331
is that obviously,

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:52.732
if there were a non-disclosure agreement

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.567
or something in a contract, it would be

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:55.401
could not publish the book.

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:56.769
Obviously, there's nothing there.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:57.570
Well, judgment call.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.371
He was able to do it.

00:03:58.371 --> 00:03:59.939
It's a judgment call.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.574
And so so that's what's happened.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.910
But look, prosecutors disagree about the

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:06.346
the weight of a case all the time.

00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:08.081
Ultimately, in this situation,

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.515
it's the DA's call.

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:10.883
You know,
just on the federal side,

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.585
Justice Department attorneys can disagree

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:13.253
about whether a case

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:14.220
should be brought or not.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.256
The attorney general's call in this case,

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:17.657
the DA made a decision.

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.158
That's the DA's job.

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:21.628
I bet the book is going to sell.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:22.929
We'll see how the investigation

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:23.763
goes from there.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.265
So that prosecutor Mark

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.900
Pomerantz resigned in protest a year ago

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.202
when his office didn't

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.637
move to indict Trump.

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:32.839
But here is what the Manhattan D.A.

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:34.907
Alvin Bragg, told CNN.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:36.609
About those accusations.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.544
He says after closely reviewing

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:39.812
all of the evidence from Mr.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.648
Palmer and his investigation.

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.683
I came to the same conclusion as several

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:46.252
senior prosecutors involved in the case.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.588
And also those I brought on.

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:50.890
More work was needed, put another way.

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:51.391
Mr.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:51.924
Pomerantz,

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.661
his plane wasn't ready for takeoff

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:55.762
Here to discuss

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:56.729
all of this is CNN

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.064
legal analyst and former federal

00:04:58.064 --> 00:04:59.799
prosecutor Eliot Williams.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:00.767
Eliot,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.403
this is obviously a person

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.104
who is selling a book.

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:06.439
But on the substance of the

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:07.440
of the allegations

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.243
here,
is there a difference of opinion here

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:11.944
just to prosecutors

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:14.080
differing on how to proceed

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.216
Or is Pomerantz right

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:19.686
that Trump got special consideration

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:21.120
because he's a former president?

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.289
I think he touched right on it.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:23.956
Abby by saying

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:25.058
there's a difference of opinion.

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:25.792
And you see this

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:26.426
all the time

00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:27.794
looking at workplaces around the country,

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:29.329
but particularly prosecutor's

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:30.396
offices around the country.

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:31.731
Now, look, let's be clear.

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:33.032
Mark Pomerantz in this book

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.134
makes an incredibly compelling case

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:35.902
for charging

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:37.470
former President Trump with a crime.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.938
But there's a few problems

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:40.006
that get glossed over,

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:40.673
frankly, in this

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.175
in the in the 60 Minutes interview.

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:43.142
Number one,

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:44.444
So much of this attention

00:05:44.444 --> 00:05:45.845
focus is on Alvin Bragg,

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.380
the current district attorney.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.047
But they don't really

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.949
you don't really talk about Cyrus

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:51.417
Vance, the former district attorney

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:54.187
who have the case going back to 2019.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.689
So if there was such a groundswell

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.124
of support for charging it,

00:05:58.124 --> 00:05:59.625
why didn't they do it back then.

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:00.593
You know there's a lot of blame

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:01.661
to go around here

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:03.529
and to a really important

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.732
point here is that if you read the book

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:07.767
there wasn't clear consensus

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:08.901
I believe in the office

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:09.836
as to whether to charge.

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:10.770
Now certainly

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.505
Pomerantz and one of his colleagues felt

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:13.139
very strongly

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:14.574
that charges should have proceeded.

00:06:14.574 --> 00:06:15.875
But there were others in the office

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:16.476
who didn't.

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:17.810
And so,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.579
you know,
I don't think it's just a difference

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:20.646
to people's opinion.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:22.315
I think it was a complicated decision

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.016
that this office was grappling with

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:24.917
and that, you know,

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.252
one of the very senior

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:26.686
and frankly,

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:28.254
very accomplished attorneys

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:29.422
just doesn't agree

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:30.990
with the decision to make.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:31.491
Yeah.

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:31.824
I mean,

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.893
it does seem like the bar,

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.528
in order to obtain a conviction

00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:36.362
of a former president,

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:39.132
certainly needs to be ironclad evidence.

00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:41.801
But the current Manhattan D.A.

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:42.502
at the moment

00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:45.138
seems to be much more focused

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:46.973
and interested in

00:06:46.973 --> 00:06:47.840
Trump's alleged

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.041
role in these hush money

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:49.675
payments

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:51.277
that were made to stop Stormy

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:52.678
Daniels Now,

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:53.946
I guess the question is,

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:56.282
could that just be an easier case

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:58.451
to prove? And if it is, why?

00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:00.653
Yes and no.

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:01.387
I think

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:01.788
to some

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.256
extent, it's an easier case to prove

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:04.424
because you have more witnesses.

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:04.824
Number one,

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:06.159
you have Stormy Daniels herself

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:07.927
who would likely come forward to testify.

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:08.461
Number two,

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:09.662
you have Michael Cohen,

00:07:09.662 --> 00:07:11.397
Trump's lawyer and former fixer

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:12.498
The challenge of Michael Cohen

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.500
is he's already got a perjury conviction.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.301
And it's sort of a

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:16.869
for lack of a better term, dirty witness.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.204
I think he'd get beaten up.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:19.672
Now, what you do have in the Stormy

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:21.641
Daniels case is a number of checks

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:22.508
that were signed,

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.643
these hush money payments,

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:24.944
I believe, frankly, even with

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:26.279
I believe what the former president's

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.180
signature himself,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.548
that you have some

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:30.917
evidence tying him to the actual crime

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.718
that might have been more complicated

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.521
and a vast real estate valuation scheme.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.756
But it's just hard to know

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:38.191
if none of us had seen

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:39.158
sort of the case

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:39.625
files that

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:41.527
both of all these prosecutors have.

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:42.929
But it's hard to know

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:44.530
exactly why one cases

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.732
seem to be moving forward and one isn't.

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:48.167
Yeah, it's a fascinating dispute

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:50.102
playing out in public here.

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:51.404
Not very typical

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:53.306
to see something like that happen.

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:54.740
Elliott Williams, thank you.

